- op: discuss  # 2893
  at: '2026-08-12T19:01:48Z'  # 2893
  by: agent  # 2893
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2893
  clock: 3  # 2893
  nonce: '2893'  # 2893
  message: "Found while verifying that the INV-todas vocabulary check did not regress the self-proof. It did not — zero vocabulary errors across all 18 claims — but the run surfaced that the artifact resolves nothing at all, for an unrelated and pre-existing reason. Filed rather than folded into the INV-todas PR: this is a scoping decision about the language-coverage gate, not a security fix, and the plan's bucket-A self-proof item depends on it."  # 2893
- op: discuss  # fb44
  at: '2026-08-12T21:18:28Z'  # fb44
  by: agent  # fb44
  actor: jgstern_agent  # fb44
  clock: 4  # fb44
  nonce: fb44  # fb44
  message: "CORRECTING THIS ITEM'S OWN FILED DIAGNOSIS. I wrote that the language-coverage\ngate is \"REPO-scoped, not CLAIM-scoped\" and proposed narrowing it as option (a).\nThat is WRONG, and I found it by measuring instead of reasoning.\n\nThe gate is ALREADY call-scoped. Measured across six repos, the set of\n\"languages emitting call edges with no io_primitives catalogue\" is:\n\n    httpx / poetry / fastapi / django   -> bash\n    pretix                              -> bash, vue\n    hypergumbo                          -> bash, csharp, solidity\n\nand hypergumbo's row is EXACTLY the {bash, csharp, solidity} the self-proof\nreported. A repo-scoped rule would have named 16 languages here, including\nmarkdown, gitignore, yaml and toml. It named three. So option (a) is a no-op:\nthere is nothing to narrow.\n\nWHAT THE BLOCKER ACTUALLY IS, and it is two separable things:\n\n 1. csharp and solidity are TEST FIXTURES — all three files are under\n    tests/fixtures/ (packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/fixtures/emission-parity/\n    csharp/Main.cs, tests/fixtures/schema-coverage-corpus/{csharp/Program.cs,\n    solidity/Contract.sol}). The boundary arm already defaults to\n    production-only scope (WI-bifob) and `classify_test_file` / `is_test_file`\n    already know how to make this call. Applying the same default to the\n    language census removes both languages from the blocker set. That is\n    option (c) from the original filing and it is the cheap, correct one.\n\n 2. bash is left, and bash is REAL: 43 tracked .sh files under scripts/ and\n    .agent/hooks/, which genuinely perform network and filesystem I/O that\n    hypergumbo cannot currently see. Excluding it would be dishonest; the\n    answer is a bash io_primitives catalogue, not a scope exclusion.\n\nSO THE REVISED PATH IS: production-scope the census (drops csharp + solidity),\nthen catalogue bash (drops the last one). Neither requires a ruling about what\na verdict means, which is what I previously said this was blocked on. Option (b)\n\"scope to languages reachable from the claim's sources\" is not needed.\n\nCONVERGENCE WITH INV-dabov: bash is the single dominant blocker for both. The\nINV-dabov gate fix (downgrade when a call-emitting language has no catalogue)\nwould make essentially EVERY repo inconclusive, because bash appears in 6 of 6\nmeasured. A bash catalogue therefore does double duty — it unblocks this item\nAND keeps INV-dabov's honest gate from swallowing every verdict — and it adds\ngenuine detection, since a shell script that curls a secret is egress the tool\ncurrently reports nothing about."  # fb44
- op: discuss  # f5a0
  at: '2026-08-13T03:57:26Z'  # f5a0
  by: agent  # f5a0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f5a0
  clock: 5  # f5a0
  nonce: f5a0  # f5a0
  message: "MEASURED AND NARROWED, NOT CLOSED. Option (c) from this item's own list\n(exclude non-production paths from the language census) is implemented; the item\nstays VIOLATED because the residual is real.\n\nBEFORE (dev d7b069b106, live run, not the cached --input of the original repro):\n  hypergumbo verify-claims . --claims docs/hypergumbo.claims.yaml\n    -> 18 INCONCLUSIVE (of 18), rc 2\n    -> \"this repo contains code in language(s) with no taint catalogue\n        (bash, csharp, solidity)\"\n\nAFTER:\n    -> 18 INCONCLUSIVE (of 18), rc 2\n    -> \"... no taint catalogue (bash)\"\n\nTHE ITEM'S OWN PREMISE WAS PARTLY WRONG, and the census is why I say so. This\nitem and the lab notebook both carried the reading that the three blocker\nlanguages are all test fixtures, so a production-scope filter would close it.\nVerified file by file:\n\n  csharp    3 files, ALL under tests/fixtures  -> dropped, correctly\n  solidity  1 file,  under tests/fixtures      -> dropped, correctly\n  bash      44 files, MOSTLY REAL              -> still blocks, correctly\n            (scripts/lib/github-api.sh, scripts/validate-agents.sh,\n             .githooks/*, .agent/hooks/*/*.sh — scripts that curl the GitHub\n             API, git push, and rm)\n\nSo (c) was never going to close this. What it does is ISOLATE the residual to\nthe one language that earns it, which is worth having on its own: the previous\nreason invited the reader to believe three catalogues were missing when two of\nthe three were fixture-only noise.\n\nWHY (c) IS DEFENSIBLE WITHOUT A RULING. It is parity with a decision already\ntaken, not a new judgment: verify_claims already discards test/fixture/\nmigration-SOURCED flows by default (include_non_production=False, WI-bifob,\n_source_scope), on the reasoning that a fixture reaching a real primitive is a\nfixture doing its job. The census asked no such question, so one fixture file\nblocked every claim while a flow out of that same file was being discarded.\nOne tool, two answers about whether a fixture counts. The fix imports\n_source_scope's classifier (now symbol_source_scope) rather than writing a\nsecond one next to the census, and the --include-non-production-sources flag\nwidens BOTH, so the two cannot disagree in either direction.\n\nSCOPED ONCE, BOTH CHECKS. Caught mid-fix and worth recording because it is\nINV-motos again: narrowing only the language census left the verdict blocking\nanyway, because _taint_blind_reason's SECOND check (compute_boundary_coverage)\nstill saw the fixture's edges. The narrowing is now applied to the edge list, so\nboth checks walk one population. compute_boundary_coverage itself is untouched —\nonly what this call site hands it — so BOUNDARY claim verdicts are unaffected.\n\nWHAT REMAINS, AND THE FORK I DECLINED TO TAKE ON THIS PASS. The blocker is now\nbash alone, and bash is genuinely uncatalogued for taint. Option (a) from this\nitem (\"scope the gate to languages REACHABLE from the claim's declared sources\")\nis filed as cheap here, and I am NOT taking it, because as stated it fails open:\nreachability would be computed by the same analysis whose completeness is in\nquestion, and a python -> bash crossing produces a SUBPROCESS BOUNDARY with no\nedge into the bash file's symbols. So \"bash is unreachable from this claim\"\nwould be true by construction for every claim, always, and the gate would become\nvacuous for exactly the language it is currently blocking on. That is the shape\nof defect INV-larol closed on the boundary side today.\n\nThere IS a sounder version worth pricing separately: all 18 claims declare their\nsources as PYTHON cmd_* handlers, so bash could only carry one of these flows if\na bash script were itself an entry point of the shipped application — which is a\nstatement about the CLAIM's declared sources, not about an absent edge. Filed\nseparately rather than decided here, because it is a weakening of a safety gate\nand belongs in its own revertable PR with its own controls."  # f5a0
- op: discuss  # 0246
  at: '2026-08-13T12:10:48Z'  # 0246
  by: agent  # 0246
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0246
  clock: 6  # 0246
  nonce: '0246'  # 0246
  message: "INVESTIGATED TO A FORK, NOT FIXED. Every remedy this item lists FAILS, each for\na different reason, and the failures are measured rather than argued. The item\nstays violated and the decision is an owner call, because the only remaining\nroutes either weaken a safety gate on a new axis or forfeit the plan's\nacceptance test.\n\nWHERE IT STANDS. Census is production-scoped (adfaaeebf2); the blocker is bash\nalone; bash is 43 tracked shell files and genuinely uncatalogued for taint.\n\n(a) SCOPE BY REACHABILITY -- FAILS OPEN, already declined on this thread and\n    re-confirmed: reachability would be computed by the same analysis whose\n    completeness is in question, and a python->bash crossing is a SUBPROCESS\n    BOUNDARY with no edge into bash symbols, so \"bash is unreachable\" is true\n    by construction for every claim, always.\n\n(a') SCOPE BY THE CLAIM'S DECLARED SOURCES -- the version I recorded as\n    \"sounder\" on this thread. IT DOES NOT HOLD, and I am retracting it rather\n    than building on it. All 18 claims declare PYTHON cmd_* handlers as sources,\n    so bash cannot ORIGINATE one of these flows -- but it can CARRY one:\n    cmd_sketch -> subprocess -> bash script -> writes to an arbitrary path is a\n    flow FROM a declared source TO host_fs that passes through bash. Declared-\n    source scoping rules out bash as an origin and says nothing about bash as\n    an intermediary, which is the case that matters.\n\n(b) CATALOGUE BASH FOR TAINT -- STRUCTURALLY BLOCKED. Measured from the shipped\n    code: the taint_sinks/ directory does not exist (retired in 51e1d232f3) and\n    EVERY built-in sink is auto-derived from io_primitives (WI-lokuv). So giving\n    bash taint sinks by the built-in path REQUIRES io_primitives/bash.yaml --\n    which ADR-0016:96 forbids, WI-javoh settled twice, and INV-larol MEASURED as\n    turning `curl -o /etc/cron.d/pwned` into a CONFIRMED \"never writes to the\n    host fs\". The recall route and the false-confirm route are the same file.\n\n(b') DECLARE BASH SINKS VIA extra_catalogs: -- FAILS OPEN IN A NEW WAY, and\n    this is the one that looked most promising so it is worth recording.\n    Project-local sinks DO flow in by that channel, and\n    `unsupported_taint_languages` is computed from the MERGED catalogue\n    (cli.py:5474), so declaring bash sinks WOULD make bash count as \"supported\"\n    and the census WOULD stop blocking. But nothing would have become\n    analysable: there is still no edge from python into bash symbols, so\n    propagation for bash finds no sources and produces nothing. The gate would\n    go quiet while the blind spot stayed exactly as large. That is dressing an\n    unanalysed language as an analysed one -- the same defect shape as (a),\n    reached by a different route.\n\nAND THE ARGUMENT THAT WOULD HAVE RESCUED (a'), WHICH ALSO FAILS. One could say\nthe python->bash crossing is itself a catalogued SUBPROCESS SINK, so the flow is\ncaught AT the boundary and seeing inside bash adds nothing. That holds ONLY for\nclaims that prohibit subprocess. It does not hold for the install claims, and\ntheir own comments say so:\n\n    install-gitleaks    Allowed: network, SUBPROCESS (extraction), ...\n                        Prohibited: HOST_FS, dev_zone\n    install-embeddings  Allowed: SUBPROCESS (pip), ...   Prohibited: HOST_FS\n    build-grammars      Allowed: SUBPROCESS (git clone, pip install)\n                        Prohibited: HOST_FS, network, ...\n\nFor those, the crossing is PERMITTED BY DESIGN and host_fs is what is forbidden\n-- so a shell script the installer invokes, writing to an arbitrary path, is\nprecisely the flow the claim forbids and precisely what bash-blindness hides.\nbuild-grammars-no-host-fs's own text even leans on it: \"the install effect is\nvia the pip subprocess\".\n\nTHE OPTION NONE OF THE ABOVE CONSIDERED, and the reason this is a fork rather\nthan a dead end. MEASURED: ZERO of the 43 tracked shell files live under\npackages/*/src -- the shipped package. The breakdown is\n.agent/hooks/ 32, scripts/ 4, .githooks/ 2, tests/fixtures 5. All of it is\nREPO TOOLING. A user who `pip install hypergumbo` never receives any of it, so\nit cannot be reached from cmd_sketch -- NOT because no edge was found, but\nbecause it is not distributed.\n\nThat is structurally different from (a) and cannot fail open the same way:\n  (a)  \"no edge found\"           -> derived from the analysis under question\n  new  \"not in the shipped artifact\" -> a fact about PACKAGING, checkable\n                                        independently of the analysis\n\nWHY IT IS STILL AN OWNER CALL AND NOT AN OBVIOUS FIX:\n  1. It is a NEW EXCLUSION AXIS on a safety gate. paths.py classifies\n     test/mock/fixture/benchmark/support and migration; it has no notion of\n     \"shipped vs repo tooling\", so this is not parity with an existing decision\n     the way the production-scope narrowing (WI-bifob) was.\n  2. The cheap implementation is a hardcoded path list (scripts/, .githooks/,\n     .agent/) -- a hand-rolled per-repo heuristic, which is the shape this\n     codebase keeps getting wrong and which would be WRONG for any repo that\n     ships its shell scripts as console entry points.\n  3. The general implementation needs a defensible \"is this file in the\n     distributed artifact\" predicate PER ECOSYSTEM (python packaging metadata,\n     go module layout, npm `files`, ...). That is a real piece of work and it\n     touches how every repo's census is scoped, not just this one's.\n  4. It would let hypergumbo's self-proof resolve while a genuine class of\n     repos -- ones that DO ship shell -- keeps a gate that this change teaches\n     to be quieter. The direction is toward fewer inconclusives, which is the\n     direction that needs the most evidence.\n\nTHE ALTERNATIVE REMAINS THIS ITEM'S OPTION (d): accept, and state in SECURITY.md\nthat the self-proof is inconclusive. Honest, cheap, and it forfeits the plan's\nbucket-A acceptance test (\"hypergumbo must USE its own io/DDG/CFG analysis to\nSHOW there can be no exfiltration, as a machine-checkable artifact anyone can\nre-run\"). Recording it as the live alternative rather than the fallback nobody\npicked.\n\nNOT DONE, and named so it is not mistaken for covered: I did not measure whether\nany corpus repo ships shell as a console entry point, which is the number that\nwould say how large the class in point 4 actually is."  # 0246
