  clock: 10  # 660e
  nonce: 660e  # 660e
  message: "OPAQUE-BOUNDARY CAVEAT KIND LANDED at dev 60ba704378, owner-authorized. This is\nADR-0016 section 4's ORIGINAL specified consumer of the fourth verdict, built\non the machinery INV-pojib landed the same day. ADR amended: both kinds are now\nimplemented (the note previously said only user_supplied_sanitizer was).\n\nRE-RUN ON THE MERGED TREE, four arms that discriminate:\n\n    launch is the sole blocker    rc 3  confirmed_with_caveats  (site NAMED)\n    real I/O, no launch           rc 0  confirmed\n    launch + a real leak          rc 1  violated\n    launch + uncatalogued module  rc 2  inconclusive\n\nThe fourth is what keeps it honest: opacity QUALIFIES only when it is the sole\nremaining blocker. Beside a genuinely uncatalogued module the silence is\nambiguous -- the reader cannot tell which gap produced it -- so the\nqualification is withheld. `qualifying_only` is derived inside\ncompute_boundary_coverage, beside the evidence, so no caller can arm it wrongly.\n\nWHAT IT ENDS: `inconclusive` was conflating \"a whole language here has no\ncatalogue, I am blind\" with \"I examined every call and understood them all;\nthree hand control to git/pip/rustup and no static analysis sees inside a\nlaunched process\". Disclaimer vs qualified opinion. Because hypergumbo launches\nprograms BY DESIGN, plain `confirmed` was permanently unreachable for its own\nself-proof -- an artifact that could never say anything.\n\nA DUPLICATE-RECORD DEFECT IN THE FIRST VERSION, found by the owner asking\nwhether the append avoids duplicate data. It did not: a claims file holding\nBOTH a boundary and a taint claim produced\n['opaque_boundary', 'opaque_boundary'] on the boundary verdict, because\nhas_taint_claims populates blind_reason for EVERY verdict. Not cosmetic --\n`caveats` is the machine surface a consumer branches on and COUNTS.\n\nTHE MIRROR OF INV-virat, HOURS APART: assign -> append is what stops a second\nwriter erasing the first, and unguarded append lets the SAME kind accumulate.\nOne fix opened its own inverse. `_merge_caveat` folds by KIND, keeps the UNION\nof entries (under-reporting unverifiable doors is the failure that matters),\nand re-renders opacity's prose so a widened list cannot leave the sentence\nsaying \"1 call site\" over two. Verified on the merged tree: DUPLICATE=False on\nboth verdicts.\n\nTWO PROCESS NOTES worth keeping. (1) A CONTROL THAT FAILED FOR THE WRONG\nREASON: my first \"no launch\" control was `return 1 + 1`, which emitted NO CALL\nEDGES AT ALL and came back inconclusive via the empty-analysis blocker;\nrebuilt with real catalogued I/O it confirms at rc 0. (2) TWO SUITE RUNS\nREPORTED IDENTICAL COUNTS and I nearly accepted it -- the anomaly was my\nmisreading (both ran the same final tree; source+tests mtime 16:59 preceded\nboth starts), confirmed by 22,577 passed + 6 skipped + 17 xfailed = 22,600 =\nexactly what collection reports.\n\nTHE SELF-PROOF DOES NOT MOVE. Still 18/18 inconclusive: its front blocker is\nbash, layer 1 of the four-layer stack. This is machinery landed ahead of its\narming event, the same ordering WI-joluk's gate was built under. INV-dabuf's\ndecision is UNCHANGED by this and still open.\n\nSuite 22,577 passed / 100% / 6 pkgs isolated / ratchet 672 delta 0."  # 660e
- op: discuss  # 1df1
  at: '2026-08-13T22:29:54Z'  # 1df1
  by: agent  # 1df1
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1df1
  clock: 11  # 1df1
  nonce: 1df1  # 1df1
  message: "LAYER-4 TRIAGE DONE (owner agreed triage-before-pricing). IT INVERTS THE\nPREMISE I GAVE: layer 4 is NOT catalogue work, and \"134 uncatalogued modules\"\nwas never a work estimate -- I quoted it as one.\n\nWHY THE MODULE COUNT MISLEADS. `_uncatalogued_external_modules` blames a MODULE\nfor the CALLS it could not classify, and its own docstring says such modules\n\"often have extensive coverage -- os carries 40 rows\". That is why os,\npathlib.Path, sys.stdout and subprocess all appear among the 134 while being\nextensively catalogued. The unit of work is the CALL, not the module.\n\nMEASURED AT CALL GRANULARITY (production-scoped, bash excluded, cached\nself-survey):\n\n    external call sites examined        9,971\n    unclassified                        9,247 across 693 DISTINCT targets\n\n    UNRESOLVED RECEIVER (module='external')  255 targets / 6,520 inst  (71%)\n    NAMED MODULE (a row could reach it)      438 targets / 2,727 inst  (29%)\n\nTHE 71% IS RECEIVER TYPING, NOT COVERAGE. The dominant targets are\nexternal.append / .get / .add / .split / .startswith / .items / .lower /\n.strip -- duck-typed method calls whose receiver was never resolved. A\ncatalogue cannot reach them: you cannot catalogue `append` without knowing what\nis being appended to. This is INV-linub, already filed, already P1, already the\nroot cause of three other symptom families.\n\nTHE 29% IS MOSTLY PURE COMPUTE. Top named targets: time.time, re.compile,\njson.dumps, hashlib.sha256, collections.defaultdict, ast.walk, ast.ast.Name,\nwarnings.warn. Rows for these would assert \"this performs no I/O\" --\nENUMERATION for completeness, not detection. Legitimate under ADR-0016's\nstdlib scope, but its value is closing the gate, not finding leaks.\n\nAND THERE IS REAL I/O HIDING IN THE 71%, which is the finding that matters:\nexternal.read_text (56), external.exists (74), external.is_file (63) are\npathlib.Path methods with untyped receivers -- genuine fs_read/fs_stat that the\nanalysis cannot see. They are invisible for a RECEIVER-TYPING reason, so they\ncome for free with INV-linub rather than needing rows.\n\nREVISED PRICING of the four-layer stack:\n  1. bash census        -> the INV-dabuf fork (scope statement / accept)\n  2. fixture languages  -> INV-sarum, small, direction pending WI-kozos\n  3. opaque launches    -> RESOLVED: the opaque_boundary caveat kind landed\n                           (dev 60ba704378); these now QUALIFY at rc 3\n  4. uncatalogued calls -> ~71% INV-linub (filed), ~29% enumeration tail\n                           (new item), NOT the large unpriced catalogue effort\n                           I described\n\nSo the only genuinely unpriced item left in the stack is the enumeration tail,\nand its purpose is gate-closing rather than recall. Filed separately.\n\nNOT SWEPT: this is python-only (the self-survey's production languages after\nbash exclusion are javascript/python/typescript, and the call targets are\noverwhelmingly python). The js/ts share of the 693 was not broken out."  # 1df1
- op: discuss  # ebdb
  at: '2026-08-15T09:40:32Z'  # ebdb
  by: agent  # ebdb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ebdb
  clock: 12  # ebdb
  nonce: ebdb  # ebdb
  message: "RE-MEASURED ON dev AFTER THE 08-14/08-15 CATALOGUE WORK. Still 18 of 18\nINCONCLUSIVE - and the cause is now a single named blocker with a measured\ncritical path, rather than \"the self-proof establishes nothing\".\n\n  python -m hypergumbo_core verify-claims . --claims docs/hypergumbo.claims.yaml\n  -> 18 claims, Counter({'inconclusive': 18}), caveats [] on every one\n\nTHE CAUSE IS ONE SENTENCE, REPEATED 18 TIMES: \"this repo contains code in\nlanguage(s) with no taint catalogue (bash), so the analysis could not look\nfor the flows\". The blindness gate is REPO-WIDE, so one uncatalogued language\nmakes every claim inconclusive regardless of what the claim is about - and\nall 18 claims' own sources are python.\n\nWHY THE BASH ROUTE DID NOT MOVE IT, measured rather than assumed. INV-vavup\nis `satisfied` and correctly so, but it shipped the BOUNDARY half only:\n\n  load_builtin_taint_catalog()\n    bash    sources=  0   sinks=  3   (redirect.> , >> , >|)\n    python  sources= 83   sinks=113\n    go      sources= 41   sinks=119\n\nUnder INV-potuf's both-halves predicate a language is taint-analysable only\nwith sources AND sinks, so bash is still unsupported. INV-vavup's own title\nsaid it: \"redirection FIRST, taint-support SECOND\". The first half shipped;\nthe second is what this item is now blocked on.\n\nAND THE SECOND HALF CANNOT BE A CATALOGUE. This is the part worth having\nmeasured before anyone writes YAML. Probe - a two-line script that is exactly\nthe flow the self-proof cares about:\n\n    secret=\"$API_KEY\"\n    echo \"$secret\" > /etc/cron.d/pwned\n\n  bash edges emitted: 1\n    calls  dst=bash:redirect:0-0:>:external_symbol\n\nThe SINK is emitted. NOTHING is emitted for the env read. So a bash taint\nSOURCES catalogue would be inert on arrival - a predicate with no call site\npassing it, which is the exact shape this repo has paid for repeatedly (io_mode\nstamped at one site; the F3 arm skipping select_by_mode). The work is in the\nANALYZER first: bash must emit an edge for a variable expansion that reads the\nenvironment. A conservative rule exists - a name expanded but never assigned in\nthe file is an environment read - and the emission shape is likely\n`module_attr_ref`, which is INV-fafol's territory (all five tree-sitter\nanalyzers anchor module_attr_ref to the FILE, so attribute-kind sources cannot\nstart a flow). Sequence those two before writing catalogue rows.\n\nTHE CRITICAL PATH, in order, with what each is worth:\n\n  1. ANALYZER: bash emits an edge for an env-var read. Without this, 2 and 3\n     are inert. Check INV-fafol first - if module_attr_ref anchors to the file,\n     the edge will exist and still not start a flow.\n  2. CATALOGUE: bash taint sources (env reads, positional params, `read`).\n     Only then does bash satisfy the both-halves predicate.\n  3. THE GATE'S DENOMINATOR, which is independent of 1 and 2 and may be the\n     cheapest real honesty win here. The uncatalogued list is: ansible, bash,\n     gitignore, html, json, markdown, toml, yaml. Six of those eight are data\n     or markup - a .gitignore cannot carry a dataflow, and blocking every claim\n     because the repo contains one is not honesty, it is noise that hides the\n     two REAL gaps (bash, ansible) behind six fake ones. Excluding\n     non-executable languages from the blind list does not weaken the gate; it\n     is the difference between \"I could not look\" and \"there was nothing to\n     look at\". NOTE: this alone does NOT flip any verdict - bash still blocks -\n     so it must not be mistaken for a fix for this item.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY WORKING, and worth stating because it is easy to read this\nitem as \"nothing works\". The sanitizer arm is live and doing real work on this\nvery repo: 2274 flows reach host_fs, 404 reach subprocess, and every one is\ncredited to hypergumbo_core.safety_zones._safety_zone_barrier on every route.\nThe machinery that would produce a `confirmed` is running; it is the\nlanguage-coverage gate in front of it that refuses, and refuses correctly.\n\nNOT CLOSED, and deliberately not downgraded. The statement is that the\nmachine-checkable safety artifact establishes nothing, and it still does not."  # ebdb
