- op: discuss  # 3901
  at: '2026-08-15T19:39:17Z'  # 3901
  by: agent  # 3901
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3901
  clock: 15  # 3901
  nonce: '3901'  # 3901
  message: "THE BLOCKER MOVED TWICE AND IS NOW A DIFFERENT, NAMED THING. Still 18/18\ninconclusive, but for a reason that is no longer about bash and is no longer\nabout language coverage at all.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED (PRs #356, #357):\n  INV-fafol  module_attr_ref now anchors to the callable that reads, so an\n             attribute source can share a caller with a sink. Measured on JS:\n             process.env.API_KEY -> fs.writeFileSync went MISSED -> FOUND\n             (1 flow -> 2). 21 of javascript's 50 derived sources are\n             attribute-kind, so this was not a bash-only prerequisite.\n  INV-jurif  bash emits an edge for an environment read, and io_primitives/\n             bash.yaml declares env_read. Measured: the two-line probe\n             (secret=$API_KEY; echo $secret > /etc/cron.d/pwned) went\n             INCONCLUSIVE -> VIOLATED.\n\nRESULT ON THE SELF-PROOF: bash is GONE from unsupported_taint_languages.\nThe list is now [ansible, gitignore, html, json, markdown, toml, yaml] and\nNONE of them blocks anything - the language gate already narrows to languages\nthat emit call edges and to production scope, and data/markup files emit none.\nSo the language-coverage story that this item was filed on is CLOSED.\n\nTHE CURRENT BLOCKER, read off the verdict rather than inferred:\n\n  \"NOT CONFIRMED: the analysis launches an external program at 81 call\n   site(s) (./scripts/auto-pr, ./scripts/bump-version, ...) and cannot see\n   what the launched program does\"\n\nThat is the OPAQUE-LAUNCH condition, and ADR-0016 section 4 already has the\nanswer for it: when named opaque launches are the SOLE remaining blocker the\nverdict should be confirmed_with_caveats (rc 3) carrying\nCAVEAT_OPAQUE_BOUNDARY, not inconclusive. That machinery is built,\nowner-authorized 2026-08-13, and wired through _require_coverage_to_confirm.\nIt is not firing.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT FIRING, measured on the self-analysis:\n\n  opaque launch sites               17\n  uncatalogued external modules    202\n\nqualifying_only is computed as `not unknown`, so a single uncatalogued module\nwithholds the qualified verdict. And the 202 are things like argparse, ast,\nasyncio, base64, builtins, collections, contextlib, copy - NON-I/O stdlib\nmodules, which are \"uncatalogued\" precisely because io_primitives catalogues\nI/O-performing modules and these do not perform I/O.\n\nSO THE FOURTH VERDICT IS UNREACHABLE ON ANY REAL REPOSITORY, which is exactly\nthe failure its own docstring says it was built to prevent: \"because\nhypergumbo launches programs BY DESIGN, `confirmed` was permanently\nunreachable for its own self-proof, making that artifact one that could never\nsay anything.\" The launch half of that was fixed; the uncatalogued-module half\nreproduces the same dead end one level down.\n\nTHE OPEN QUESTION, and it is a DESIGN question rather than a defect, which is\nwhy I stopped here rather than changing it. `unknown` currently means \"a call\ninto a module I hold no I/O rows for\". For argparse that is literally true and\npractically vacuous. Candidate readings, none of them free:\n\n  (a) A module with no I/O rows is not an unknown, it is a module that does no\n      I/O. Cheapest, and makes the fourth verdict reachable - but it converts\n      \"I have no rows for this\" into \"this does nothing\", which is the\n      absence-of-evidence move this project keeps refusing elsewhere.\n  (b) Distinguish \"catalogued as doing no I/O\" from \"never examined\", which\n      needs a positive no-I/O declaration the catalogue does not have today.\n      Honest, and real work.\n  (c) Leave it, and accept that the self-proof can never exceed inconclusive.\n\nDIRECTION WARNING, which is why this is not mine to decide alone: every one of\nthese moves verdicts TOWARD confirming. The plan's decision point 4 says stop\nand report on the expensive direction, and this is that direction. What is NOT\nin doubt is that the current state is also wrong - a verdict that can never be\nanything but inconclusive establishes nothing, which is this item's own\nstatement.\n\nNOT SWEPT: whether the 17 opaque launch sites measured here reconcile with the\n81 the verdict reports (different populations - one is the cached\nself-analysis, the other the live run). Reconcile before acting on either."  # 3901
- op: discuss  # 51e5
  at: '2026-08-15T20:09:54Z'  # 51e5
  by: agent  # 51e5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 51e5
  clock: 16  # 51e5
  nonce: '51e5'  # 51e5
  message: "OWNER RULING, 2026-08-15, verbatim: \"B - but it ought to be limited strictly\nto stdlib - for non-stdlib I am pretty sure we already figured out a separate\nway to deal with that\".\n\nSo the three-way question posted above is CLOSED. Option (a) \"no rows means no\nI/O\" is REJECTED and option (c) accept-and-disclose is not being taken. The\nchosen route is (b): a POSITIVE no-I/O declaration, so that a module nobody has\nexamined stays `unknown` and only a reviewed one clears the gate.\n\nTHE SCOPE LIMIT IS PART OF THE RULING, not a detail: strictly stdlib. That is\nparity with the standing io_primitives ruling (ADR-0016 section 27 - the\ncatalogue is stdlib-scoped; third-party is USER-SUPPLIED via overlays), so the\nno-I/O declaration inherits the same boundary as the I/O declarations it sits\nbeside. A third-party module with no rows therefore REMAINS unknown and\ncontinues to withhold the qualified verdict - which is the correct direction,\nbecause the overlay mechanism is how a user asserts anything about a dependency\nthey ship.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT DECIDE, and must not be read as deciding:\n  - It does not say the 202 measured modules are all stdlib. That is a PREMISE\n    OF MY OWN FILING and is being verified before any row is written; the\n    ruling's scope limit only binds the ones that are.\n  - It does not reconcile the 17-vs-81 launch-site discrepancy noted at the end\n    of the previous entry (cached self-analysis vs live run). Still open, still\n    to be reconciled before the qualified verdict is trusted.\n\nDIRECTION, restated so the next reader does not have to re-derive it: this moves\nverdicts TOWARD confirming, which is the expensive direction under the plan's\ndecision point 4. The safeguard the owner chose is that each step is backed by a\nhuman-reviewable declaration rather than an inference from the tool's own gaps."  # 51e5
- op: discuss  # f103
  at: '2026-08-15T20:28:47Z'  # f103
  by: agent  # f103
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f103
  clock: 17  # f103
  nonce: f103  # f103
  message: "TWO FURTHER OWNER RULINGS, same session, both verbatim.\n\nRULING 2 - THIRD-PARTY MODULES GET AN OVERLAY, AND IT SHIPS AS AN EXAMPLE:\n\"if the 202 contains even one genuine third-party module, then we also make the\noverlay for that/those third-party modules since we already started doing that\nanyway for hypergumbo and if we haven't we should because we should include it\nas an example for users\".\n\nANSWER TO THE CONDITION, measured: YES - 52 genuine third-party modules (yaml,\nnumpy, tree_sitter*, textual.*, starlette.*, rich.*, tomli, ruamel.yaml,\nbcrypt, onnxruntime, sentence_transformers, cairosvg, watchfiles,\nplatformdirs, proquint, tokenizers, vite, ...). AND WE HAD NOT: docs/\nio-primitives-overlays/ ships two EXAMPLES (python-http-clients.yaml,\ngo-web-frameworks.yaml) but docs/hypergumbo.claims.yaml's `extra_catalogs:`\ncarries sources/sinks/sanitizers and NO `io_primitives:` key at all, so the\nself-proof consumes zero overlays. The owner's suspicion was correct.\n\nRULING 3 - AN OVERLAY MAY DECLARE A MODULE ENUMERATED: \"i mean what else would\nit do? you have a better idea? if not, then of course it may.\"\n\nCONTEXT THAT PROMPTED IT: `load_catalog` REFUSES `stdlib_module_completeness`\nin an overlay (io_boundary.py:1307) on the reasoning that a completeness entry\n\"grants strictly MORE than\" primitive rows - it vouches for every call the\ncatalogue could not classify, not just one named surface (INV-zosun). The\nconsequence, which is why this had to be raised rather than worked around: a\nthird-party module can NEVER leave the uncatalogued set no matter how many\noverlay rows are written, so ruling 2 alone improves DETECTION and moves the\nverdict not at all.\n\nTHE ONE REFINEMENT I AM FOLDING IN, offered because the owner asked for a\nbetter idea rather than for agreement: keep the grant, fix the NAME. The key is\nliterally `stdlib_module_completeness`, and once an overlay may declare `numpy`\nor `tree_sitter` enumerated the key holds non-stdlib modules under a name that\nsays otherwise - a field silently carrying two populations, which is the\nfundamental-concept-audit shape. Widen the concept to `module_completeness`\n(the honest spelling of \"modules whose I/O surface this catalogue has\nenumerated\"), keep `stdlib_module_completeness` accepted so no shipped\ncatalogue or user overlay breaks, and KEEP the per-module dated audit record -\nthe value of the mechanism is that a human wrote down that they looked, and a\nblanket switch would destroy exactly that.\n\nRESULTING WORK ORDER, three revertable PRs:\n  1. INV-vokog  the launch/blindness double-count (measured 212 -> 137\n                uncatalogued; verdict UNCHANGED, stated so it is not mistaken\n                for a fix)\n  2. ruling 1   stdlib module_completeness audit rows\n  3. rulings 2+3 overlay may declare completeness; hypergumbo's own\n                third-party overlay authored and wired into extra_catalogs,\n                doubling as the shipped user example"  # f103
